<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perth, Scotland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a>, near the royal centre of Scone. A royal burgh from the 12th century, it was often the seat of the Scottish court and Parliament in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and James I was murdered there in 1437. It was a leading trading burgh, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a>'s sermon there in 1559 set off the riots that began the Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perth-scotland.jpg" alt="Perth, Scotland" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Val Vannet · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perth_Concert_Hall,_Perth,_Scotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> stands on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a>, on a natural mound raised slightly above the flood plain. The area has been occupied since the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers, and nearby standing stones and circles date from about 4000 BCE. Close to the city is Scone Abbey, which formerly housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-of-scone">Stone of Scone</a>, on which the kings of Scots were traditionally crowned. The name comes from a Pictish word for a wood or copse, related to the Welsh perth, meaning hedge or thicket. In the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> its inhabitants called it St John's Toun, after the church of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The frequent residence of the royal court gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> the reputation of a capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a> granted royal burgh status in the early 12th century. The city became one of the richest burghs in the country, trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and the Baltic and importing Spanish silk and French wine. It grew from a plan of two parallel streets, High Street and South Street, linked by vennels. In 1437 King James I was assassinated in Perth by followers of Walter Stewart, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>, at Blackfriars church.</p><p>In 1559 a sermon by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> in St John's Kirk was followed by the sacking of the Greyfriars and Blackfriars houses, two of the city's four monastic institutions. Jacobite supporters occupied the city in 1689, 1715 and 1745. Perth Academy was founded in 1760, and linen, leather, bleach and whisky became major industries. The first railway station opened in 1848. In 1829 Sir George Murray, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>, asked that the new Swan River Colony in Western Australia be named Perth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> is the administrative centre of Perth and Kinross and the historic county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>. It has been known as the Fair City since Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a> published The Fair Maid of Perth in 1828. St John's Kirk, much altered, keeps a 15th-century brass candelabrum imported from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, a rare survival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Its arms, recorded on a seal of 1378, show a paschal lamb holding a saltire, and the North Inch and South Inch were given to the city in 1377 by King Robert III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perth, Scotland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal burghs such as Berwick, <b>Perth</b>, Aberdeen and Edinburgh held monopolies of foreign trade, exporting wool, hides, fish and later coal and salt to the Low Countries, the Baltic and France; from the fifteenth century a Scottish staple port in the Netherlands, from 1541 at Veere, handled much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
